CENSUS {"python": "/oscar/scratch/sboughan/step1b/venv-fetch/bin/python", "registry": ["Acrobot", "AntmazeGiantNavigate", "AntmazeGiantStitch", "AntmazeLargeExplore", "AntmazeLargeNavigate", "AntmazeLargeStitch", "AntmazeMediumExplore", "AntmazeMediumNavigate", "AntmazeMediumStitch", "AntmazeTeleportExplore", "AntmazeTeleportNavigate", "AntmazeTeleportStitch", "BabyAIGoToLocal", "CartPole", "CraftaxClassic", "Crafter", "FetchPickAndPlace", "FetchPickAndPlaceDense", "FetchPickAndPlaceDict", "FetchPush", "FetchPushDense", "FetchPushDict", "FetchReach", "FetchReachDense", "FetchReachDict", "FetchSlide", "FetchSlideDense", "FetchSlideDict", "Highway", "HumanoidmazeGiantNavigate", "HumanoidmazeGiantStitch", "HumanoidmazeLargeNavigate", "HumanoidmazeLargeStitch", "HumanoidmazeMediumNavigate", "HumanoidmazeMediumStitch", "Intersection", "Merge", "MiniGridDoorKey", "MiniGridEmpty", "MiniGridFourRooms", "MiniGridKeyCorridor", "MiniGridLavaCrossing", "MiniGridPOVFetch", "MiniHack", "MountainCar", "MountainCarContinuous", "MujocoInvertedDoublePendulum", "MujocoInvertedPendulum", "MujocoPusher", "MujocoReacher", "Parking", "Pendulum", "Piecewise", "PiecewiseZones", "PointMazeLarge", "PointMazeMedium", "PointMazeOpen", "PointMazeUMaze", "PointmazeGiantNavigate", "PointmazeGiantStitch", "PointmazeLargeNavigate", "PointmazeLargeStitch", "PointmazeMediumNavigate", "PointmazeMediumStitch", "PointmazeTeleportNavigate", "PointmazeTeleportStitch", "PushT", "Racetrack", "Roundabout", "SimplePointMaze", "TwoRoom", "TwoRoomTarget"], "n": 72, "import_errors": {"carracing": "ImportError: CarRacing-v3 cannot be reset and rendered: DependencyNotInstalled: Box2D is not installed, you can install it by run `pip install swig` followed by `pip install \"gymnasium[box2d]\"`. This env needs BOTH `box2d-py` (for the simulator that produces its states) and `pygame` (for the reference renderer parity is measured against); `pip install swig` then `pip install \"gymnasium[box2d]\"`.", "dmcontrol": "ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'dm_control'", "griddly": "ImportError: griddly is not importable (ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'griddly'). The atlas is composed of tiles griddly's own Vulkan renderer draws, so there is no degraded mode. `pip install griddly==1.6.7` \u2014 the only release, from 2023-03-15; upstream's last commit is 2024-04-07 and there is no successor.", "humanoidbench": "ImportError: the HumanoidBench family is measured and complete on fidelity (17 cells, VISUAL tier, mean 2.6-8.4/255, SSIM 0.845-0.931, 437x the official renderer at batch 64) but is NOT registered by default: mujoco_warp's batched render is not bit-reproducible inside a long conformance session, so 2-3 of ~660 tests fail every run on test_determinism and test_frame_i_is_independent_of_batch_size, by 1-2/255, on a different cell each time. Set STABLE_ENVS_HUMANOIDBENCH=1 to register it anyway. See docs/research/humanoidbench.md", "jumanji": "ImportError: the Jumanji family builds its per-pixel atlas by CALLING the official matplotlib viewer, so it cannot render without the reference package: missing jumanji, jax, matplotlib. The envs were measured in /mnt/vast/home/randall.balestriero/venvs/se-jumanji, built with a CLEAN `python -m venv` \u2014 a --system-site-packages venv breaks jax's CUDA plugin discovery. See docs/research/jumanji.md", "lunarlander": "ImportError: LunarLander-v3 cannot be reset and rendered: DependencyNotInstalled: Box2D is not installed, you can install it by run `pip install swig` followed by `pip install \"gymnasium[box2d]\"`. This env needs BOTH `box2d-py` (for the simulator that produces its states) and `pygame` (for the reference renderer parity is measured against); `pip install swig` then `pip install \"gymnasium[box2d]\"`.", "metaworld": "ImportError: the Meta-World family ray-casts the Sawyer's meshes with mujoco_warp's batch raytracer, which this interpreter cannot run: missing metaworld; mujoco is 3.13.0 and mujoco_warp requires >= 3.11 (metaworld itself pins mujoco==3.3.0, which is why it is installed with --no-deps). The envs were measured in /mnt/vast/home/randall.balestriero/venvs/se-fetch; see docs/research/metaworld.md and the `metaworld` extra in pyproject.toml", "overcooked": "ImportError: jaxmarl is not importable (ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'jaxmarl'). The atlas IS jaxmarl's own `_render_tile` output \u2014 nothing here re-derives it \u2014 so there is no degraded mode. `pip install jaxmarl` (measured against jaxmarl 0.1.0 / jax 0.4.38) INTO A CLEAN VENV: a `python -m venv --system-site-packages` venv breaks jax's CUDA plugin discovery.", "pgx": "ImportError: the PGX family needs pgx (which brings jax), resvg_py and Pillow, and this interpreter cannot provide them: ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pgx'. The envs were measured in /mnt/vast/home/randall.balestriero/venvs/se-pgx, built with a CLEAN `python -m venv` -- NOT --system-site-packages, which breaks jax's CUDA plugin discovery. See docs/research/pgx.md", "procgen": "ImportError: procgen is not installed; `pip install procgen` provides both the assets this renderer packs and the reference env parity is measured against", "robocasa": "ImportError: no compiled RoboCasa kitchen is cached under /users/sboughan/.cache/stable-envs/robocasa. robocasa pins mujoco == 3.3.1 and mujoco_warp needs >= 3.11, so the kitchen has to be built by a different interpreter and cached:\n\n    conda run -n robocasa python examples/robocasa_visual/dump_trajectory.py --kitchen L52S03 --steps 200 --res 256 --out /users/sboughan/.cache/stable-envs/robocasa\n\nIt needs robocasa's 24 GB asset library. Set STABLE_ENVS_ROBOCASA_CACHE to point somewhere else. See docs/research/robocasa.md", "sokoban": "ImportError: gym-sokoban is not installed. The seven 16x16 tile images ARE this env -- the frame is a gather from them and nothing here re-derives them -- so there is no degraded mode to fall back to. `pip install gym-sokoban`."}, "file": "/oscar/scratch/sboughan/step1b/stable-envs/stable_envs/__init__.py", "declarations": {"CartPole": {"name": "cartpole", "reference_id": "CartPole-v1", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 2, "default_size": 600, "state_names": ["x", "theta"], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "CartPole", "module": "stable_envs.envs.classic_control", "doc": "CartPole-v1. Render state ``[x, theta]`` \u2014 2 of the observation's 4 dims.\n\nDraw order, from the source: cart quad, pole quad, axle disc, then ``hline`` for the track, which\nis painted LAST and therefore over the cart. The cart is axis-aligned, so its ``aapolygon`` takes\nSDL_gfx's solid ``hline``/``vline`` short-circuit and is not anti-aliased at all; only the pole is.\n\n``step`` takes 2 discrete actions \u2014 0 push left, 1 push right \u2014 as one float per environment,\nfloored. Dynamical state ``[x, x_dot, theta, theta_dot]``, Euler at ``tau = 0.02``; tracks the\nreference to **4.4e-14 over 100 steps, 1.1e-16 after one**.", "has_step": true, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["gfxdraw"]}, "Pendulum": {"name": "pendulum", "reference_id": "Pendulum-v1", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 2, "default_size": 500, "state_names": ["theta", "torque"], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "Pendulum", "module": "stable_envs.envs.classic_control", "doc": "Pendulum-v1. Render state ``[theta, torque]``.\n\n``torque`` is ``last_u``: not part of the observation, but it is drawn, so it is part of the render\nstate. See the module docstring for why it reduces to a 228-entry sprite table and why ``u = 0``\nneeds no separate flag.\n\n``step`` takes one CONTINUOUS torque in [-2, 2]. Dynamical state ``[theta, theta_dot, torque]``, and\nstepping is what makes the third render dimension move on its own: the torque a policy asks for is\nthe torque that gets drawn. Tracks the reference to **2.8e-13 over 100 steps, 2.2e-16 after one**.", "has_step": true, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["gfxdraw"]}, "Acrobot": {"name": "acrobot", "reference_id": "Acrobot-v1", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 2, "default_size": 500, "state_names": ["theta1", "theta2"], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "Acrobot", "module": "stable_envs.envs.classic_control", "doc": "Acrobot-v1. Render state ``[theta1, theta2]`` \u2014 2 of the observation's 6 dims.\n\nTwo traps in the source, both silent. ``xys = np.array([[0,0], p1, p2])[:, ::-1]`` **swaps x and\ny**, so link 2 hangs off ``(p1_y, p1_x)``; and ``p2`` is computed but never drawn, because only two\n``thetas`` are zipped against three points. The ground rule is ``pygame.draw.line``, which\nTRUNCATES its float y (363.6364 -> row 363) \u2014 rounding puts the whole line one row off, 1000 px a\nframe, which looks like a rasterizer bug rather than a coordinate one.\n\n``step`` takes 3 discrete actions \u2014 torque -1, 0, +1 \u2014 as one floored float. Dynamical state\n``[theta1, theta2, dtheta1, dtheta2]``, one RK4 step at ``dt = 0.2``; tracks the reference to\n**4.4e-13 over 100 steps, 1.1e-16 after one**. The widest spread in the family and the fewest\nbit-identical steps (15.4%), which is what a chaotic double pendulum is supposed to do to two\ntrajectories that started one ulp apart.", "has_step": true, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["gfxdraw"]}, "MountainCar": {"name": "mountain-car", "reference_id": "MountainCar-v0", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 1, "default_size": 600, "state_names": ["position"], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "MountainCar", "module": "stable_envs.envs.classic_control", "doc": "MountainCar-v0. Render state ``[position]`` \u2014 one number for a 400x600 frame.\n\nThe terrain is 99 ``pygame.draw.aalines`` segments, a sub-pixel AA path unrelated to gfxdraw's and\nnot reimplemented; it is also a constant of the environment rather than of the state, so it is\nrasterised once by pygame and cached. That is exact by construction and free per frame. Everything\nthe state moves \u2014 the car quad and its two wheels \u2014 is pure gfxdraw and exact by the rules in\n``render/gfxdraw``. The flag is drawn AFTER the car in the reference, so it cannot join the baked\nlayer; it is gfxdraw too, so it does not need to.\n\n``step`` takes 3 discrete actions \u2014 0 push left, 1 coast, 2 push right \u2014 as one floored float.\nDynamical state ``[position, velocity]``; tracks the reference to **3.3e-16 over 100 steps,\n5.4e-20 after one**, the tightest in the family because one step is three float64 operations and a\ncosine.", "has_step": true, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["gfxdraw"]}, "MountainCarContinuous": {"name": "mountain-car-continuous", "reference_id": "MountainCarContinuous-v0", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 1, "default_size": 600, "state_names": ["position"], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "MountainCarContinuous", "module": "stable_envs.envs.classic_control", "doc": "MountainCarContinuous-v0. Nearly two envs for the price of one \u2014 but not quite.\n\n``continuous_mountain_car.render`` is byte-identical to ``mountain_car.render`` apart from one\ncomment (checked by diffing the two sources), which makes it very tempting to reuse the renderer\nunchanged. That is wrong by 23,984 pixels across 64 frames, because the flag is drawn from\n``self.goal_position`` and the two envs do not agree on it: **0.45 here, 0.5 for MountainCar-v0**,\nwith a source comment attributing the difference to a third-party port. Identical draw code over a\ndifferent constant is exactly the class of error a \"looks the same\" check misses and a pixel diff\ncatches immediately.\n\nThe same trap runs through the physics, one level deeper: ``step`` here takes one CONTINUOUS force\nin [-1, 1] scaled by ``power``, and it computes in **float32** where MountainCar-v0 computes in\nfloat64 (see ``_dynamics``). Seeded from a state the reference has already stored, agreement is\n**exact \u2014 exact_frac 1.0, max deviation 0.0 over 16 x 100 steps**; seeded from a freshly reset\nfloat64 state the reference's own first step is in a different dtype regime and the trajectory\ndiffers by up to one float32 ulp of position (1.2e-07).", "has_step": true, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["gfxdraw"]}, "CraftaxClassic": {"name": "craftax-classic", "reference_id": "Craftax-Classic-Pixels-v1", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 127, "default_size": 16, "state_names": ["block_r0c0", "block_r0c1", "block_r0c2", "block_r0c3", "block_r0c4", "block_r0c5", "block_r0c6", "block_r0c7", "block_r0c8", "block_r1c0", "block_r1c1", "block_r1c2", "block_r1c3", "block_r1c4", "block_r1c5", "block_r1c6", "block_r1c7", "block_r1c8", "block_r2c0", "block_r2c1", "block_r2c2", "block_r2c3", "block_r2c4", "block_r2c5", "block_r2c6", "block_r2c7", "block_r2c8", "block_r3c0", "block_r3c1", "block_r3c2", "block_r3c3", "block_r3c4", "block_r3c5", "block_r3c6", "block_r3c7", "block_r3c8", "block_r4c0", "block_r4c1", "block_r4c2", "block_r4c3", "block_r4c4", "block_r4c5", "block_r4c6", "block_r4c7", "block_r4c8", "block_r5c0", "block_r5c1", "block_r5c2", "block_r5c3", "block_r5c4", "block_r5c5", "block_r5c6", "block_r5c7", "block_r5c8", "block_r6c0", "block_r6c1", "block_r6c2", "block_r6c3", "block_r6c4", "block_r6c5", "block_r6c6", "block_r6c7", "block_r6c8", "mob_r0c0", "mob_r0c1", "mob_r0c2", "mob_r0c3", "mob_r0c4", "mob_r0c5", "mob_r0c6", "mob_r0c7", "mob_r0c8", "mob_r1c0", "mob_r1c1", "mob_r1c2", "mob_r1c3", "mob_r1c4", "mob_r1c5", "mob_r1c6", "mob_r1c7", "mob_r1c8", "mob_r2c0", "mob_r2c1", "mob_r2c2", "mob_r2c3", "mob_r2c4", "mob_r2c5", "mob_r2c6", "mob_r2c7", "mob_r2c8", "mob_r3c0", "mob_r3c1", "mob_r3c2", "mob_r3c3", "mob_r3c4", "mob_r3c5", "mob_r3c6", "mob_r3c7", "mob_r3c8", "mob_r4c0", "mob_r4c1", "mob_r4c2", "mob_r4c3", "mob_r4c4", "mob_r4c5", "mob_r4c6", "mob_r4c7", "mob_r4c8", "mob_r5c0", "mob_r5c1", "mob_r5c2", "mob_r5c3", "mob_r5c4", "mob_r5c5", "mob_r5c6", "mob_r5c7", "mob_r5c8", "mob_r6c0", "mob_r6c1", "mob_r6c2", "mob_r6c3", "mob_r6c4", "mob_r6c5", "mob_r6c6", "mob_r6c7", "mob_r6c8", "player_facing"], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "CraftaxClassic", "module": "stable_envs.envs.craftax", "doc": "Craftax Classic's world layer: a 7x9 block window, the player, and one mob per tile.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": []}, "Crafter": {"name": "crafter", "reference_id": "CrafterReward-v1", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 80, "default_size": 64, "state_names": ["cell[0,0]", "cell[0,1]", "cell[0,2]", "cell[0,3]", "cell[0,4]", "cell[0,5]", "cell[0,6]", "cell[1,0]", "cell[1,1]", "cell[1,2]", "cell[1,3]", "cell[1,4]", "cell[1,5]", "cell[1,6]", "cell[2,0]", "cell[2,1]", "cell[2,2]", "cell[2,3]", "cell[2,4]", "cell[2,5]", "cell[2,6]", "cell[3,0]", "cell[3,1]", "cell[3,2]", "cell[3,3]", "cell[3,4]", "cell[3,5]", "cell[3,6]", "cell[4,0]", "cell[4,1]", "cell[4,2]", "cell[4,3]", "cell[4,4]", "cell[4,5]", "cell[4,6]", "cell[5,0]", "cell[5,1]", "cell[5,2]", "cell[5,3]", "cell[5,4]", "cell[5,5]", "cell[5,6]", "cell[6,0]", "cell[6,1]", "cell[6,2]", "cell[6,3]", "cell[6,4]", "cell[6,5]", "cell[6,6]", "cell[7,0]", "cell[7,1]", "cell[7,2]", "cell[7,3]", "cell[7,4]", "cell[7,5]", "cell[7,6]", "cell[8,0]", "cell[8,1]", "cell[8,2]", "cell[8,3]", "cell[8,4]", "cell[8,5]", "cell[8,6]", "inv_health", "inv_food", "inv_drink", "inv_energy", "inv_sapling", "inv_wood", "inv_stone", "inv_coal", "inv_iron", "inv_diamond", "inv_wood_pickaxe", "inv_stone_pickaxe", "inv_iron_pickaxe", "inv_wood_sword", "inv_stone_sword", "inv_iron_sword", "daylight"], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "Crafter", "module": "stable_envs.envs.crafter", "doc": "Crafter's 64x64 observation: a 9x7 lit world window plus the 2x9 inventory strip.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["tilemap"]}, "FetchReach": {"name": "fetch-reach", "reference_id": "FetchReach-v4", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 0.99, "parity_note": "TWO declared residuals, measured separately, both structural to the backend rather than to this scene. (1) THE ROBOT'S 18 MESHES ARE SHADED PER PIXEL AND MuJoCo SHADES THEM PER VERTEX. MuJoCo draws meshes as triangles with Gouraud interpolation of vertex colours; an analytic per-pixel Blinn-Phong evaluation lands within a level or two of it and not on it. The robot is 8.9% of the frame and carries essentially all of the interior residual -- 0.8% of interior pixels, at an interior mean of 0.009/255, i.e. the disagreement is one level where it exists at all. This is the same limit the OGBench port records and refuted three ways (Gouraud over MuJoCo's own tessellation scored WORSE than plain per-pixel there), and it bites here because a Fetch mover is 9% of the frame rather than 20 pixels. (2) THE TRANSLUCENT MOCAP MARKERS ARE NOT DRAWN. robot0:mocap carries a 1 cm cube at alpha 0.7 and three 2 m crosshair bars at alpha 0.1; mujoco_warp's shader reads only color[0..2] and packs a constant 255, so it has no alpha path at all and would paint them opaque saturated green. Measured against a reference with them hidden: they are 1.2-1.5% of ALL pixels (mean 0.32/255) and 0.000002 of INTERIOR pixels -- a translucent bar over a flat surface leaves no flat 3x3 neighbourhood, so none of it reaches the interior metric. That is also why mocap is not in the state: it reaches no other pixel. What is NOT in these numbers, because it was found and fixed: the float->uint8 quantisation rule (mujoco_warp truncates, GL rounds), worth interior 0.791 -> 0.9988 on its own. The static 91% of the frame -- floor, table, skybox -- is exact by construction, because it is baked through MuJoCo itself.", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 480, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "FetchReach", "module": "stable_envs.envs.fetch", "doc": "gymnasium-robotics FetchReach-v4 (swm/FetchReach-v3). State is qpos plus the 3-D goal; the sparse variant differs from its siblings in the reward and the observation container, neither of which reaches the renderer, so it shares the Reach plate and model.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["mjwarp"]}, "FetchPush": {"name": "fetch-push", "reference_id": "FetchPush-v4", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 0.99, "parity_note": "TWO declared residuals, measured separately, both structural to the backend rather than to this scene. (1) THE ROBOT'S 18 MESHES ARE SHADED PER PIXEL AND MuJoCo SHADES THEM PER VERTEX. MuJoCo draws meshes as triangles with Gouraud interpolation of vertex colours; an analytic per-pixel Blinn-Phong evaluation lands within a level or two of it and not on it. The robot is 8.9% of the frame and carries essentially all of the interior residual -- 0.8% of interior pixels, at an interior mean of 0.009/255, i.e. the disagreement is one level where it exists at all. This is the same limit the OGBench port records and refuted three ways (Gouraud over MuJoCo's own tessellation scored WORSE than plain per-pixel there), and it bites here because a Fetch mover is 9% of the frame rather than 20 pixels. (2) THE TRANSLUCENT MOCAP MARKERS ARE NOT DRAWN. robot0:mocap carries a 1 cm cube at alpha 0.7 and three 2 m crosshair bars at alpha 0.1; mujoco_warp's shader reads only color[0..2] and packs a constant 255, so it has no alpha path at all and would paint them opaque saturated green. Measured against a reference with them hidden: they are 1.2-1.5% of ALL pixels (mean 0.32/255) and 0.000002 of INTERIOR pixels -- a translucent bar over a flat surface leaves no flat 3x3 neighbourhood, so none of it reaches the interior metric. That is also why mocap is not in the state: it reaches no other pixel. What is NOT in these numbers, because it was found and fixed: the float->uint8 quantisation rule (mujoco_warp truncates, GL rounds), worth interior 0.791 -> 0.9988 on its own. The static 91% of the frame -- floor, table, skybox -- is exact by construction, because it is baked through MuJoCo itself.", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 480, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "FetchPush", "module": "stable_envs.envs.fetch", "doc": "gymnasium-robotics FetchPush-v4 (swm/FetchPush-v3). State is qpos plus the 3-D goal; the sparse variant differs from its siblings in the reward and the observation container, neither of which reaches the renderer, so it shares the Push plate and model.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["mjwarp"]}, "FetchSlide": {"name": "fetch-slide", "referenc