_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "AGENT-PIXEL PARITY (supersample 2, against MuJoCo at its default offsamples=4, 25 cells x 64 states). Every disagreement is inside the agent's own silhouette: the baked plate is exact by construction, non-agent pixels are 99.88-99.99% bit-identical, and that residual is the reference's multisampling bleeding agent colour into neighbouring wall/floor pixels. On agent-interior pixels (eroded by one, so coverage is not in play) the shader reaches 48-84% within 1/255 for pointmaze (mean 1.2-2.5/255), 0-51% for antmaze and 0-3% for humanoidmaze. The legged numbers are not a shading result: at the official whole-maze camera the ant is 12-70 px and the humanoid 4-21 px of 50176, and eroding leaves as few as 1 interior pixel, so their agreement is a statement about a silhouette under the reference's coverage rule. Against a NON-multisampled reference the same shader reaches 92.4% within 1/255 over 13,069 pooled agent-interior pixels (mean 0.88/255), which localises most of the remaining gap in MSAA sample positions -- GPU-specific, not recoverable by fitting -- rather than in shading. Three mechanisms were tried and are recorded so they are not retried: the fixed-function composition (FIXED - specular is additive and white and the texture modulates the whole primary colour, which took wall tops from 13/255 worst to 2/255 and made vertical wall faces bit-exact); Gouraud interpolation over MuJoCo's sphere/capsule tessellation (REFUTED - ten (slices, stacks, phase, diagonal) variants all scored 0.0-0.2% exact against 1.6% for plain per-pixel, which is also what sub-pixel triangles predict); and a mis-shaped specular lobe (REFUTED - a 24-knot piecewise-linear lobe fitted under four light/specular geometries, headlight directional or point crossed with Blinn or Phong, could not explain floor and agent with one curve).", "state_dim": 2, "default_size": 224, "state_names": 